/E (The most complex 2.5D materials are hybrid multidimensional architectures that integrate components of different dimensionalities. These might combine 2D sheets with 0D quantum dots \(tiny semiconductor particles that exhibit size-dependent properties\), 1D nanotubes \(rolled sheets that form molecular wires\), or even 3D nanostructures. An example includes MXene sheets decorated with platinum nanoparticles, which make highly efficient catalysts for fuel cells by maximizing the active surface area while keeping electrical conductivity through the 2D framework.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
