/E (No. The real problem is that we have thousands of models that can explain the available data. There are roughly five key pieces of evidence for dark matter, and many models are capable of satisfying them. So the main question becomes, what general properties must dark matter have to fit these observations? These broad requirements can be met by a vast number of models, which makes it very difficult to pinpoint the correct one. The challenge is not a lack of ideas but rather having too many of them and not knowing which one truly works. We can only figure it out by doing experiments where we can select a set of models over others saying that nature has cast it's vote.)
